02044677734375, 0.036285400390625, -0.022796630859375, -0.00589752197265625, 0.005184173583984375, 0.00408172607421875, 0.0175018310546875, 0.0001271963119506836, -0.01316070556640625, -0.0080718994140625, 0.0013332366943359375, -0.01239776611328125, -0.0177154541015625, -0.0009965896606445312, 0.0287933349609375, 0.0214996337890625, -0.0237884521484375, 0.01093292236328125, 0.0127410888671875, -0.01483154296875, 0.01410675048828125, 0.0201873779296875, 0.0021800994873046875, 0.0305023193359375, -0.0006341934204101562, 0.02642822265625, -0.01275634765625, -0.004444122314453125, 0.005519866943359375, 0.01715087890625, -0.024505615234375, 0.01910400390625, 0.0235595703125, -0.034820556640625, 0.0168609619140625, -0.0133209228515625, -0.02532958984375, 0.01509857177734375, 0.0082244873046875, 0.01464080810546875, -0.057220458984375, 0.0199432373046875, -0.007404327392578125, 0.00946807861328125, 0.00014162063598632812, 0.005229949951171875, 0.0103302001953125, 0.0052947998046875], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**\n1. Introduction and Motivation\n**\n\nWe rigorously analyze how the temporal order of synaptic inputs affects the firing behavior of sparse threshold-reset networks in the fast-synapse limit. Our main contribution is a precise mathematical demonstration that, as synaptic delays vanish, the order of excitatory and inhibitory inputs can cause discontinuous changes in network output, even when the total input converges weakly. This work clarifies the limitations of weak convergence and mean-field approaches in capturing order-sensitive dynamics, and provides explicit conditions under which input order determines firing. Our results establish a concrete link between input order and firing outcomes in a widely used class of models: leaky integrate-and-fire (LIF) neurons with threshold-reset, sparse random networks with Dale's law, and strictly positive synaptic delays.\n\n**Scope and Limitations**\n\nOur analysis applies specifically to networks of leaky integrate-and-fire neurons with hard threshold-reset dynamics, strictly positive synaptic delays, and random directed connectivity satisfying a typewise-mixing condition (see Section 3.4). We do not address models with continuous or stochastic thresholding, gap junctions, or instantaneous synapses. The fast-synapse limit, in which synaptic kernels become arbitrarily brief, is a mathematical idealization; while real synapses have finite kinetics, our results are relevant for cases where synaptic timescales are much shorter than membrane or network timescales. The biological plausibility and limitations of this regime are discussed in Section 5.\n\n**1.1. Biological Context and Relevance**\n\nPrecise spike timing and input order are critical in real neural circuits. For example, in hippocampal pyramidal neurons, the relative timing of excitatory and inhibitory postsynaptic potentials can determine whether a neuron fires or remains silent [1]. In the auditory brainstem, microsecond differences in input order underlie sound localization [2]. Our model captures this phenomenon by showing that, even for infinitesimal delays, the order of excitation and inhibition can switch a neuron's response from firing to silence. While real synaptic delays are not infinitesimal, our results provide a theoretical underpinning for experimental findings that emphasize the importance of input order in neural computation, and highlight the limitations of mean-field or rate-based models that ignore precise timing. However, we note that the fast-synapse limit is an idealization; in biological systems, synaptic and dendritic integration may partially mitigate such order effects.\n\n**\n2. Notation, Definitions, and Illustrative Examples\n**\n\nLet $\\mathbb{R}$ denote the real line, and let $\\mathcal{M}(\\mathbb{R})$ denote the space of finite signed Borel measures on $\\mathbb{R}$. For $\\mu \\in \\mathcal{M}(\\mathbb{R})$, the Jordan decomposition is $\\mu = \\mu^+ - \\mu^-$, where $\\mu^+, \\mu^-$ are finite nonnegative Borel measures.\n\n**Definition 1 (Weak Convergence and Componentwise Weak Convergence).**  \nA sequence $(\\mu_N)_{N\\geq 1}$ in $\\mathcal{M}(\\mathbb{R})$ converges weakly to $\\mu$ if, for every bounded continuous function $f:\\mathbb{R}\\to\\mathbb{R}$,\n\\[\n\\int f\\,d\\mu_N \\to \\int f\\,d\\mu \\quad \\text{as } N\\to\\infty.\n\\]\nFor signed measures, this is equivalent to requiring that both $\\mu_N^+ \\to \\mu^+$ and $\\mu_N^- \\to \\mu^-$ weakly; this is called componentwise weak convergence. Weak convergence captures convergence of the overall distribution of input times and strengths, but does not preserve the precise order of individual events.\n\n*Example:* Two spike trains, one with an excitatory spike at $t=0$ and an inhibitory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$, and the other with the order reversed, both converge weakly to the same measure as $\\varepsilon \\to 0$, but their effects on a threshold neuron can differ.\n\n**Definition 2 (Order Sensitivity).**  \nA neuron or network is said to be *order sensitive* in the fast-synapse limit if, for two input spike trains whose associated input measures converge weakly to the same limit, the output (e.g., whether the neuron fires) differs for the two protocols in the limit as synaptic delays vanish.\n\n*Example:* If an excitatory spike just precedes an inhibitory spike, the neuron may fire; if the order is reversed, it may not.\n\n**Definition 3 (Typewise-Mixing).**  \nLet $G_N$ be a random directed graph on $N$ vertices, partitioned into $C$ synaptic classes (e.g., excitatory, inhibitory). For each class $c$, let $S_c$ be the set of neurons of type $c$. The network is *typewise-mixing* if, for every $i$ and every $c$, the empirical distribution of incoming edges to neuron $i$ from $S_c$ converges in total variation to a fixed probability measure as $N\\to\\infty$. This ensures that each neuron receives a representative sample of inputs from each class, avoiding pathological connectivity patterns.\n\n*Example:* In an Erd\u0151s\u2013R\u00e9nyi random graph with fixed connection probabilities for each class, the typewise-mixing condition holds.\n\n**Glossary of Symbols**\n- $K_\\varepsilon$: Synaptic kernel, a $C^\\infty$ nonnegative function supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$ with $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$.\n- $a, b$: Pulse masses for excitatory and inhibitory spikes, respectively.\n- $\\varepsilon$: Synaptic delay parameter.\n- $V(t)$: Membrane potential at time $t$.\n- $\\theta$: Firing threshold.\n- $V_{\\mathrm{rest}}$: Resting potential.\n- $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$: Reset potential after spike.\n- $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$: Refractory period.\n- $\\lambda$: Leak rate.\n- $w_{ij}$: Synaptic weight from neuron $j$ to $i$.\n- $d_{ij}$: Synaptic delay from neuron $j$ to $i$.\n\n**\n3. Model Specification and Assumptions\n**\n\n**3.1. Neuron Model**\n\nWe consider a leaky integrate-and-fire neuron with membrane potential $V(t)$ governed by\n\\[\n\\frac{dV}{dt} = -\\lambda (V(t) - V_{\\mathrm{rest}}) + I_{\\mathrm{syn}}(t),\n\\]\nwhere $\\lambda\\geq 0$, $V_{\\mathrm{rest}}$ is the resting potential, and $I_{\\mathrm{syn}}(t)$ is the total synaptic input. When $V(t)$ reaches threshold $\\theta$, the neuron emits a spike, $V$ is reset to $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$, and held at $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$ for a refractory period $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}>0$.\n\n**3.2. Synaptic Kernels and Fast-Synapse Limit**\n\nEach synaptic event is modeled by a kernel $K_\\varepsilon \\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$, nonnegative, supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$, and normalized so that $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. The fast-synapse limit is the regime $\\varepsilon \\downarrow 0$, in which $K_\\varepsilon$ converges weakly to the Dirac delta $\\delta_0$. However, weak convergence does not preserve the order of arrival of inputs: two spikes at $t=0$ and $t=\\varepsilon$ become indistinguishable as $\\varepsilon \\to 0$, even though their order can have dramatic effects on threshold-based dynamics.\n\n**3.3. Spike Processing Protocol**\n\n- Spikes are processed in strict temporal order.\n- After a spike, the membrane potential is clamped at $V_{\\mathrm{reset}}$ for $\\tau_{\\mathrm{ref}}$.\n- All synaptic delays are strictly positive.\n\n**3.4. Network Structure and Dale Compatibility**\n\n- Each neuron is either purely excitatory or purely inhibitory (Dale's law).\n- The network is a random directed graph $G_N$ on $N$ vertices, partitioned into finitely many synaptic classes.\n- The typewise-mixing condition (Definition 3) holds.\n\nThese assumptions ensure well-posedness of the dynamics, prevent pathological instantaneous feedback, and reflect biological constraints [1,2].\n\n**\n4. Main Theorem: Order Sensitivity in the Fast-Synapse Limit\n**\n\nWe focus on the effect of two temporally close spikes, parameterized by a small delay $\\varepsilon>0$ and pulse masses $a>0$ (excitatory), $b>0$ (inhibitory):\n\n- *Excitatory-first protocol*: Excitatory spike at $t=0$, inhibitory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n- *Inhibitory-first protocol*: Inhibitory spike at $t=0$, excitatory spike at $t=\\varepsilon$.\n\nEach spike is modeled as $a K_\\varepsilon(t)$ (excitatory) or $-b K_\\varepsilon(t-\\delta)$ (inhibitory, with $\\delta=0$ or $\\varepsilon$). The total input is the sum of the corresponding kernels.\n\n**Theorem 1 (Order Sensitivity in the Fast-Synapse Limit).**  \nLet $K_\\varepsilon \\in C^\\infty(\\mathbb{R})$ be nonnegative, supported on $[0,\\varepsilon]$, and $\\int K_\\varepsilon = 1$. Let $a"}}